-state basis so far we've  passed 16 bills 10 states 650 000 people  are on a pathway to get out of prison in  the next five years that shouldn't be  there now by the way there's people that  do belong in prison yeah and you know  this is about getting the people that  shouldn't be in prison out of prison and  how we keep communities safe and also  not waste taxpayer pay our money we're  doing all that so and me this is  personal for you i want people to  understand what you went through can you  briefly describe it i remember once  hearing you say a judge or a prosecutor  with a pen can cause just as much damage  and be just as deadly as someone with a  gun uh when i when i made that statement  i actually made it from sitting in the  curt room watching a judge just  sentenced men and women over simple  violations like weed  marijuana you smoke marijuana on  probation and you know you got sent to a  state prison and you know your kids grew  up without a mother for three years i  just didn't thought i didn't think that  decision was the right decision to be  made out of common sense and then you  know later on in my life it started to  affect my life  me uh i was a lucky one to escape it but  many others like me were unable to  escape it and i was able to be in those  prison with those people face to face  while being successful uh seeing those  people marched for me uh michael stood  up for me we sat down in that visiting  room i told him about my case and  everything how i got in a situation  stood up to all my wrongs or whatever i  felt i done wrong  and expressed him about uh how uh  probation basically almost rejected me  from having a fair shot  at life that the average young black man  should have coming up so you know i  wanted to help and make a change with  that what we're doing now you mentioned  the marijuana violation that's one way  that people can get back into jail when  they're on parole by the term right what  are some of the other ways the sort of  simple violations that can send a person  back to prison uh i could just give you  a shoe like if uh gal you was taking  kids to disney world tomorrow and i  wanted to take my son tomorrow and if i  was on probation uh if i went to disney  world with you and not having like a  sexual crime or anything like that where  you couldn't be around kids if i went to  disney world with you i would be  arrested just for taking my child to  disney world if i didn't contact my  probationer in a certain manner my  probation officer uh you could be  arrested for not being hired for a job  you know you can't time when you would  be hired for a job you know especially  if you have a felony on your record  you could be sent to prison for not  completing school  for not completing the program or just  uh getting a kid up from school i used  to remember voicemails yeah or meek's  probation officer yeah he'd say can i  pick my kid up from school ten minutes  marie lives in new jersey he lived in  pennsylvania she'd say no if you pick  your kid up i will send you to prison  that is crazy in real time on real live  tv i like to express that like where i'm  from in philadelphia it's a lot of  violence for the young black and brown  so my son would attend school in new  jersey he lived in a suburban area  because he  uh  he he grew above poverty through my  music success and he's a smart kid yes a  very a very smart child so you know me  being a rapper traveling in the world  some days i would want to go home and  take my child to school of course it's  kind of almost impossible to make that  decision and call a probation officer so  i would take the risk of taking my child  to school so he could have a feeling of  his father being there so some of those  days earlier you mentioned jobs and the  reform alliance it also puts together uh  job fairs yeah why is this an important  aspect of probation reform  uh i believe it's a important aspect  because if you're out here and being a  a person who contributes to society i  think you should work for your own money  uh you know having a felony is already a  strike against you right uh where you'll  be at a disadvantage so we try to  provide jobs to people who  like myself if i don't know if you guys  call it a second chance i live by god so  i don't live by chances but a second  chance give them a chance to rebuild  their self  and uh you know  td jake said something interesting me  once he told me that he's never seen  somebody who had to go to work the next  day who was robbing somebody  so the importance of having a job and  having a purpose michael i've heard you  say this system is broken that it starts  with the system is broken and that's  really what we want to fundamentally fix  when i saw meat go to prison for not  committing a crime for literally um  popping wheeling on a motorcycle and got  sentenced to two to four years on  probation yeah once once um you know  jay-z and desiree perez and myself work  together to get him out of prison we  also we have to fix the underlying  system you can't fix this one at a time  there's a systemic issue that has there  are millions of people today on  probation that shouldn't be on probation  and for all now by the way there's  millions that are on probation and  parole should be there and they need  help we need to be rebuilt for those who  are doing the right thing though the  system is broken yeah it's not rewarding  their decisions of turning their life  around i know i know we have to go but  can you just tell us something about the  new music that's coming yeah you seem to  be in a really good place in your life  are you yeah i'm in i'm in a real hungry  place in my life so you know we had  september coming up i've been out of uh  uh out the way just working on business  turning my business models up on the  inside before i dive back in and really  so is it dropping soon what are we  talking about yeah uh trey young uh a  lot of the guys the twins a l